Short:        Full-screen media centre launcher for AGS
Author:       Brian King
Uploader:     Brian King
Type:         util/wb
Version:      [VERSION]
Requires:     Kickstart 3.1+, OCS/ECS/AGA or RTG
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.1

AmigaMCE is a fast, full-screen media centre for the Amiga, in the spirit
of Kodi, RetroArch and Windows Media Center. It is a drop-in companion to
Amiga Game Selector (AGS): copy the AmigaMCE drawer anywhere on an AGS
installation and double-click it.

Features in this release
------------------------
- Browses the AGS menu tree (Games, Demos, Disk Magazines, Favourites)
  with hierarchical categories, never one giant flat list.
- Shows each title's AGS fact sheet and screenshot, decoded through the
  installed picture datatypes. On screens 640 or more wide, game lists
  have a preview pane beside them: the selected game's screenshot and
  its fact sheet (the key lines on 200/256-line screens).
- Launches titles through their AGS .run scripts, honouring the AGS
  Expert, ExpertPerm and Adult settings, then returns to AmigaMCE.
  While a game runs AmigaMCE frees everything it can (its screen, lists,
  fonts and artwork, and the libraries it does not need, which the
  system then unloads) and comes back on the same entry afterwards.
- Screen mode chosen in Settings: 320x200 NTSC, 320x256 PAL,
  640x200/256 hires, 640x400/512 hires laced (AGA), 640x480, 800x600
  or 1024x768 RTG (when the card has them), or
  Automatic (hires on AGA, lowres otherwise). Never uses Extra Half-Brite.
- Smooth-scrolling, flicker-free text pages (fact sheets, Credits,
  System information) with a scroll bar when the text is longer than the page.
- Hires screens (laced or not) use a dense layout: more of each list fits.
- Big RTG screens scale the whole layout up. Settings > Style chooses
  the look there: Pixel (the pixel artwork, 2x on 1024x768) or Smooth
  (anti-aliased text, icons and logo at exactly the screen's size:
  1.25x on 640x480, 1.56x on 800x600, 2x on 1024x768; on 16-bit and
  deeper RTG screens).
- Pictures: browses Media:Images (folders first, each picture with its
  format), with the selected picture or a folder's first picture in the
  preview pane. A folder that holds only pictures is marked with a play
  arrow: Fire plays it as a slideshow, Right opens it. The viewer shows
  pictures full screen on AmigaMCE's own screen, so the display never
  changes mode, at a whole scale that keeps their pixels' shape. On
  native and 256-colour screens a picture gets the whole palette while it
  is shown, its own colours exactly when it has no more than the screen
  can show, and the info bar is drawn in the picture's own colours.
- Keyboard, mouse (including wheel) and joystick/CD32 pad control.

Mods, Videos, Music and Radio are listed but arrive in a later
version. About holds the credits and a System information
page showing what AmigaMCE detected (CPU, Kickstart, chipset, screen).

Controls
--------
  Up/Down      move             Shift+Up/Down  page
  Fire/Return  play a game, open a folder or setting (or double-click)
  Right        open a folder; on a game, into its details pane when
               that scrolls (lowres: the game's own page)
  Left/Esc     back             Esc at menu    press twice to quit
  A-Z          jump to letter   Right mouse    back
  Mouse wheel  scroll           Scroll bar     click to page, drag to scroll

  Pictures     Fire/Return views a picture or plays a folder marked with
               the play arrow as a slideshow; Right or a double-click
               opens a folder.
  Viewer       Left/Right (or a click) previous/next, Fire/Return/Space
               slideshow on/off (5 seconds a picture), Esc or the right
               mouse button back.

Installation
------------
Copy the AmigaMCE drawer to your hard drive. Keep the Fonts drawer next
to the program. From the floppy (ADF), unpack the archive on it, e.g.
  lha x DF0:amigamce-[VERSION].lha Work:
which makes the AmigaMCE drawer there. If AGS is set to boot straight into its menu, quit AGS
to Workbench first, then double-click AmigaMCE.

Settings > Debug shows the system CPU load and AmigaMCE's memory use in
the header, and writes a timing log to AmigaMCE.log next to the program
(or RAM:AmigaMCE.log if that drawer is read-only).

The screen mode is saved as ENVARC:AmigaMCE/ScreenMode and the style
as ENVARC:AmigaMCE/Style. Keep the Fonts drawer complete: Fonts/amce-smooth
holds the smooth style for each RTG screen height. If a mode does
not suit your display, delete ENVARC:AmigaMCE/ScreenMode to go back
to Automatic.

